# Keyless reference for every variable this repo reads. Real values never live in a
# plaintext file — they go into the per-package `.env.local.enc`, encrypted by dotenvx.
#
#   bun install
#   cp .env.example apps/web/.env.personal    # fill in, stays git-ignored
#   cp .env.example packages/db/.env.personal
#
# `.env.personal` is a plaintext local-only overlay that layers on top of (and
# overrides) the encrypted file. Nothing else reads it and it is never committed.
#
# To put a value into the committed encrypted file instead — the normal path for
# anything a teammate also needs — add the key manually at the right line in the
# target `.env.local.enc`, then:
#
#   dotenvx set MY_VAR "value" -f apps/web/.env.local.enc
#
# Order matters: `dotenvx set` on a key that does not exist yet appends it to the
# bottom, breaking the line-alignment the files rely on. Encrypting needs only the
# public key in the file header, so you can do this without the private key.
#
# Which file owns which variable:
#
#   apps/web/.env.local.enc      NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_*, SENTRY_DSN, NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN
#   apps/api/.env.local.enc      DATABASE_URL, CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS, SENTRY_DSN
#   packages/db/.env.local.enc   DATABASE_URL, DIRECT_URL  (Prisma CLI only)
#
# Decryption keys live in a git-ignored `.env.keys` next to each one. New checkout:
# ask for those three keys, or re-encrypt with your own.

# ─── Neon Postgres ────────────────────────────────
# sslmode=verify-full, not require: `pg` treats them identically today, but a future
# major adopts libpq semantics where `require` skips certificate verification. Neon
# presents a publicly trusted cert, so verify-full costs nothing and can't silently weaken.

# Pooled (PgBouncer) — what the apps run on.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=verify-full"

# Direct — Prisma CLI only, so it belongs in packages/db. Migrate needs session-level
# advisory locks, which the pooler drops.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://user:password@host.region.aws.neon.tech/dbname?sslmode=verify-full"

# apps/web reads neither of the above. It has no database access at all — it goes through
# apps/api, and an ESLint rule blocks importing @repo/db.

# ─── API origin (apps/web) ────────────────────────
# Declared in the `client` bucket of apps/web/src/lib/env.ts (t3-env), which is also
# where a new web variable goes — `client` for anything the browser reads, `server`
# for anything it must not.
#
# Where the browser and the Server Components reach apps/api. Inlined into the client
# bundle, so it must be a URL the browser can resolve — not a container hostname. Whatever
# origin this names must also appear in the API's CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL="http://localhost:4000"

# ─── API (apps/api) ───────────────────────────────
# Validated by apps/api/src/config/env/validation.ts — key order there mirrors the
# order here and in apps/api/.env.local.enc.
#
# PORT is deliberately absent, like NODE_ENV. Ports are a code default for local dev
# (4000, in validation.ts) and the deployed container's business everywhere else
# (Dockerfile `ENV PORT`, App Platform `http_port`). Setting it in a dotenv file adds a
# third source that can disagree with the other two, and the symptom is a container that
# passes its health check on a port nothing is routed to.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS="http://localhost:3000"

# ─── PostHog (apps/web) ───────────────────────────
# Browser-only: product analytics belongs where the person and session are. The API
# has no PostHog integration on purpose.
#
# The project token is the write-only ingest key (`phc_…`), inlined into the client
# bundle by design. It is not the POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY in .env.personal, which is a
# read/write credential for the MCP server and never belongs in a `.enc`.
# Find it at: PostHog -> Settings -> Project -> Project API key.
#
# Leave the key unset and posthog-js no-ops — a checkout still runs.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY="phc_your_project_token"

# The UI origin, for toolbar and dashboard links only. Ingest goes through the /ingest
# rewrite in apps/web/next.config.ts, which is what keeps adblockers from dropping it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST="https://us.posthog.com"

# ─── Sentry (apps/web and apps/api) ───────────────
# Not secrets — a DSN only grants event ingest. Leave them unset and the SDK no-ops,
# which is what you want for a throwaway checkout.
#
# The two apps report to two different Sentry projects, so `SENTRY_DSN` holds a
# different value in each `.env.local.enc` — `javascript-nextjs` for web,
# `comp-trial-api` for the API. Same key name, do not copy one into the other.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is web-only: it is inlined into the browser bundle, while the bare one
# is read at runtime by the Next server/edge configs and, in the API, by
# src/instrument.ts. The API deliberately reads it from process.env rather than
# EnvService — instrument.ts runs before the Nest container exists.
SENTRY_DSN="https://<key>@<org-id>.ingest.us.sentry.io/<project-id>"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN="https://<key>@<org-id>.ingest.us.sentry.io/<project-id>"

# Build-time only, and a real secret — uploads source maps so production stack traces
# aren't minified. Set in Vercel, not locally, which is why it is in no `.enc` file.
# Generate at: https://comp-ai-trial.sentry.io/settings/auth-tokens/ (scope: project:releases)
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=""

# NODE_ENV is deliberately absent. Next.js and the runtime own it; pinning it in a
# dotenv file is how a build ends up prerendering in the wrong mode.
